                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cc212be0-1d07-3e60-83fb-931f25135d2a",
                        "entry_id": "2cc50443-cb1c-3332-a764-11864e109ea0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Relation sémantique entre mots d'une même langue, partitive et hiérarchisée : Le méronyme A d'un substantif B est un substantif qui désigne une partie de B."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c78bfa85-b3cc-4816-89ff-19769315c020",
                                "sense_id": "cc212be0-1d07-3e60-83fb-931f25135d2a",
                                "text": "La relation du mot « bras » au mot « corps » est une méronymie."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3c8b830f-993d-4115-a9f3-ad6f0640c377",
                                "sense_id": "cc212be0-1d07-3e60-83fb-931f25135d2a",
                                "text": "« Toit » est un méronyme de « maison »."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a3f9513f-d4c3-4c47-b273-b2cdb3ae1c2d",
                                "sense_id": "cc212be0-1d07-3e60-83fb-931f25135d2a",
                                "text": "Pour ces derniers sont identifiées des classes lexicales définies en extension : organisateurs, noms dénotant la méronymie, verbes dénotant la segmentation ou le rassemblement."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "be0deaf0-a726-440d-aaf9-ab453b97b48b",
                        "source_entry_id": "2cc50443-cb1c-3332-a764-11864e109ea0",
                        "source_sense_id": "cc212be0-1d07-3e60-83fb-931f25135d2a",
                        "target_lang_word": "meronymy",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "meronymy",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c313c15d-910d-3bc2-8acd-cdb22baf8f94",
                                        "entry_id": "107b8fa6-a4ff-3951-adb1-a7a217d0ea4e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The relationship of being a constituent part or member of something; a system of meronyms."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "742aa091-0261-4ae0-9a76-2c564b0bc824",
                                                "sense_id": "c313c15d-910d-3bc2-8acd-cdb22baf8f94",
                                                "text": "This relationship of meronymy is controversial for various reasons. First, there are several types of meronymy, such as functional meronymy, where one concept is a functional part of another (e.g. FINGER-HAND) or more general part-whole relations, where the part and the whole exist as a continuous entity (e.g. FLAME-FIRE). Secondly, there are diverging opinions as to whether meronymy should be treated as a semantic primitive in the sense of s[yn]onymy, antonymy, and hyponymy."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "082330a3-fff1-4b31-84b5-497e90386ef1",
                                                "sense_id": "c313c15d-910d-3bc2-8acd-cdb22baf8f94",
                                                "text": "But whereas hyponymy is a member–class relation, reflecting a taxonomy or conceptual hierarchy, meronymy is a part–whole relation, reflecting the existence of complex structures in concrete reality."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "45ea44e0-8c45-46d7-9e60-22262e1e3e3a",
                                                "sense_id": "c313c15d-910d-3bc2-8acd-cdb22baf8f94",
                                                "text": "Possession, like meronymy, is described in English (and equivalently in other languages) with the verb to have (A millionaire has money) and the line between possession and part-having is fuzzy at best.[…]Priss (1998) suggests that meronymy might be formalized as an attribution relation, such that has-a-handle-for-a-part would be an attribute of hammer and cup. Thus, the case for separating attribution and possession from meronymy is not strong."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
